      ":question/id": "202c529d-9cba-5685-92d4-a9dce18272d8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-z+6 & =-z+6 \\\\ -z+6+z & =-z+6+z \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3z+7 & =-4z+7 \\\\ -3z+7+4z-7 & =-4z+7+4z-7 \\\\ z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3z+7=-4z+7$ has the unique solution $z=0.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+7 & =2z+6 \\\\ 2z+7-2z & =2z+6-2z \\\\ 7 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $2z+7=2z+6$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2z+7=2z+6$ only.",
