    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "afe84848-d206-5a46-8f71-46189f0e5416",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $-2x=-2x-3$\n\nII. $-5x+2=2x+5$\n\nIII. $-9x+7=-9x-5$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =-2x-3 \\\\ -2x+2x & =-2x-3+2x \\\\ 0 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x=-2x-3$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+2 & =2x+5 \\\\ -5x+2-2x-2 & =2x+5-2x-2 \\\\ -7x & =3 \\\\ x & =- \\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-5x+2=2x+5$ has the unique solution $x=- \\frac{3}{7}.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x+7 & =-9x-5 \\\\ -9x+7+9x & =-9x-5+9x \\\\ 7 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-9x+7=-9x-5$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "77d8bb74-2ca0-547f-b4b0-8a471247c39e",
